course: everyday
lesson: 4
title: Climbing trees and playgrounds
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Climbing is great fun and makes you strong, and the most important rule is «three points of contact»: always keep three of your four hands and feet on the tree or frame, and move only one at a time.
explain: Then if one hand or foot slips, the other three still hold you.
explain: In a tree, test each branch before you trust it, step close to the trunk where branches are thickest, and stay off dead, dry or cracked branches, which can snap.
explain: Climb only as high as you can safely climb back down, because coming down is usually harder than going up.
explain: On a playground, take off your bike helmet and anything with cords, because they can catch on the equipment; a girl in Melbourne tucks her hoodie strings away before she goes up the climbing net.
explain: A boy in New Zealand climbed onto a thin branch high in a tree to reach a ball; the branch snapped and he fell, breaking his leg in two places.
explain: A girl in Alberta climbed the outside of the playground slide while other children were sliding down; one bumped into her, she fell onto the ground and hit her head.
fact: Three points of contact means keeping two hands and a foot, or two feet and a hand, on what you climb.
fact: You should move only one hand or foot at a time when you climb.
fact: Dead branches are dry and brittle and can snap without warning.
fact: Branches are thickest and strongest close to the trunk.
fact: Climbing down is often harder than climbing up because you cannot see where your feet go.
fact: Wet branches and wet climbing frames are slippery.
fact: A bike helmet should be taken off before you play on a playground, because its straps can catch.
fact: Cords, drawstrings and scarves on clothes can catch on playground equipment and pull tight around your neck.
fact: Soft ground like wood chips, sand or rubber under a climbing frame makes falls less dangerous than concrete.
fact: Tree climbing should stay well away from power lines.
fact: An adult should know where you are when you climb a tree.
fact: Falls from climbing frames, trees and playground equipment cause most playground injuries.
fact: Falling from more than about your own height onto a hard surface can break bones or injure your head.
fact: If someone falls from a height and does not move, do not move them; shout for an adult and get help straight away.
exercise: What does «three points of contact» mean? | Three of your hands and feet always hold on | You need three friends to climb with you | You climb three steps at a time | You touch the tree three times before you start
exercise: How many hands or feet should you move at one time when you climb? | One | Two | Three | All four
exercise: Why is three points of contact safer? | If one slips, the other three still hold you | It makes you climb much faster than your friends | It makes the tree grow stronger | It makes you lighter
exercise: Which branch is safest to stand on? | A thick, living branch near the trunk | A thin branch near the tip | A dry, dead branch | A branch with a long crack along the middle
exercise: Why are dead branches dangerous? | They are brittle and can snap | They are full of sap | They bend too much and bounce | They are always very wet
exercise: Why is climbing down often harder than climbing up? | You cannot easily see where your feet go | Gravity is stronger on the way down than up | Trees get taller while you climb | Your hands get smaller
exercise: How high should you climb? | Only as high as you can safely climb down | As high as the tree goes, right to the top | Higher than all your friends | Until the branches get thin
exercise: What should you do with your bike helmet before playing on a climbing frame? | Take it off | Loosen the strap | Wear it backwards | Tie it to the frame
exercise: Why are drawstrings on clothes a danger on playgrounds? | They can catch on the equipment | They make you too warm | They make you slip | They make a noise and scare the birds
exercise: Which ground is best under a climbing frame? | Wood chips, sand or rubber | Concrete | Tarmac | Smooth paving stones or bricks
exercise: A tree near your house has electric wires running through it. What should you do? | Never climb it | Climb only the lower half | Climb it with rubber shoes | Climb it only on dry days
exercise: The climbing frame is wet after the rain. What should you remember? | Wet bars are slippery, so take care or wait | Wet bars give a better grip | Rain makes metal softer to hold | Water makes you lighter, so you can climb higher
exercise: Who should know when you go to climb a tree? | An adult who looks after you | Nobody at all | Only your younger brother | Only the birds in the tree, who live there
exercise: How should you test a branch before standing on it? | Push on it gently while still holding on | Jump on it hard with both feet | Look at its colour from far away | Tap it once with one finger
test: A boy in Florida reaches for a high branch with both hands while one foot hangs free. What is wrong? | He has only one point of contact left | He is climbing too slowly | He should use his teeth too | Nothing, that is the best way
test: Ava finds a grey branch with no leaves in summer. Should she stand on it? | No, it may be dead and could snap | Yes, grey branches are the strongest | Yes, if she jumps on it fast | Only if it is high up
test: Sam climbed easily up a tall tree but is scared to come down. What lesson does this teach? | Only climb as high as you can climb down | Always climb as fast as you can, before you get scared | Never look up while climbing | Trees are easier to climb in the dark
test: Which is true about playground safety? | Bike helmets should come off on climbing frames | Helmets make climbing frames safer for everyone | Hoodie strings are safe on slides | Concrete is the best ground under swings
test: Why do builders and firefighters on ladders also use three points of contact? | So they still hold on if one hand or foot slips | Because ladders have three legs | It is only a habit with no reason behind it at all | So they can carry two buckets
test: Where on a tree is a branch usually strongest? | Close to the trunk | Out at the thin end | Right at the top | On the dead side
test: What could happen if a scarf caught on a slide while a child went down? | It could pull tight around the child's neck | The slide would go faster | The scarf would keep her warm | Nothing, scarves just slip off the slide on their own
test: A girl in Auckland climbs a tree after rain. What should she know? | The bark and branches may be slippery | Rain makes branches stronger | Wet trees are easier to climb, since bark gets sticky | Rain makes the tree shorter
test: Why is a playground with rubber matting safer than one with concrete? | A fall onto rubber is softer | Rubber stops children from climbing high | Rubber makes the frames lower | Concrete is too cold to play on
test: up the tree, can i stand on that grey dry branch | No, dead branches can snap | Yes, dry branches are the strongest | Yes, if you jump on it first | Yes, the thin end is safest
test: which bit of the branch do i stand on, near trunk or the end | Near the trunk, it is strongest | Out at the end, it bends nicely | In the middle of the air | Wherever the leaves are thickest
test: still got my bike helmet on, gonna go on the climbing frame | Take it off first, the straps can catch | Keep it on, it is safer there | Only take it off at the slide | Loosen the strap a bit and keep it on your head
test: can we climb the big tree near the power lines | No, stay well away from power lines | Yes, if you don't touch the wires with your hands | Yes, rubber shoes keep you safe | Yes, if the wires look old
test: Jake fell outta the tree and isn't moving, what do i do | Do not move him; shout for an adult and get help | Pull him up so he can sit | Pour water on his face | Run home and say nothing about it to anyone
test: branch is kinda thin but the ball's right there, go for it? | No, thin branches snap, ask an adult | Yes, if you move very fast | Yes, if you bounce on it to test it | Yes, if you hold on with one hand
test: Sam says it's fine to climb when it's raining, grip's the same. true? | No, wet branches and bars are slippery | Yes, rain makes them sticky | Yes, wet wood holds better | Yes, if you wear thick socks over your shoes
test: Ethan's sister fell outta the big oak cos the branch snapped, the ambulance came and everything | The real lesson is to avoid dead or thin branches and keep three points of contact | The real lesson is that ambulances come for every tiny fall | It proves only girls fall out of trees | It means oak trees are the only trees that are dangerous
test: someone said a kid got hanged on the slide by his hoodie string, like for real, at our park | Whether or not it was our park, drawstrings really can catch, so take them off or tuck them in | It is surely made up, so drawstrings are always safe | It means slides are only safe for children with no hoodies at all | It proves you should wear your bike helmet on the slide
test: Ruby got stuck up the tree and cried till the fire brigade came lol, everyone's gonna know | Do not spread it; getting stuck can happen to anyone and she was scared | Spread it, because the fire brigade makes it a great story | Spread it, so she learns not to climb ever again | Spread it, but say it was someone else's sister
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the principle of three points of contact? | Two hands and one foot, or two feet and one hand, remain upon the object being climbed | Three persons must be present whenever a tree is climbed | The climber must rest upon three branches simultaneously | Three limbs are moved together whilst one remains still
exam: Identify the principal reason for which dead branches ought not to bear the weight of a climber. | Being dry and brittle, they are liable to snap without warning | Being heavy, they bend the tree towards the ground | Being green, they are excessively slippery | Being thick, they cannot be grasped by the hand
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the surface beneath playground climbing equipment. | Wood chips, sand or rubber diminish the gravity of injury in a fall, as compared with concrete | Concrete affords the greatest protection against injury in a fall | The surface is of no consequence whatever to injury | Grass is the only surface on which falls cause no injury
exam: It being given that a bicycle helmet is worn upon arrival at a playground, determine the appropriate course of action. | The helmet should be removed prior to play, as its straps may become entangled | The helmet should be retained, as it protects the head upon climbing frames | The helmet should be worn upon the back by its straps | The helmet should be fastened more tightly before climbing
